>
  <p>
    Your tent is usually the heaviest item in your pack and the one you rely on
    most when the weather turns. It is also the purchase beginners find hardest,
    because listings are full of numbers that are difficult to compare.
  </p>
  <p>
    For most people starting out, a freestanding, double-wall two-person hiking
    tent is the safest first choice, even if you mostly walk alone. This guide
    explains why, and how to judge weight, space and weather protection before
    you buy.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A two-person tent gives a solo hiker room for their pack, and two hikers
        a snug but workable fit.
      </li>
      <li>
        Rough weight bands: ultralight under 1.5 kg, lightweight 1.5-2.5 kg,
        standard 2.5-3.5 kg.
      </li>
      <li>
        Freestanding double-wall tents are the easiest to pitch and handle
        condensation best.
      </li>
      <li>
        A three-season tent covers the vast majority of Australian hiking.
      </li>
      <li>
        Pitch it at home before your first <a
          href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list">overnight hike</a
        >.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Why choose a 2 person hiking tent?</h2>
  <p>
    Tent capacity ratings assume people lying shoulder to shoulder with very
    little else inside. A one-person tent fits one person and not much more; a
    two-person tent fits two people closely, or one person plus a pack, boots
    and wet gear kept out of the rain. Our <a
      href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list">overnight hike packing list</a
    > shows how much gear that is.
  </p>
  <p>
    That flexibility is why a two-person tent is the most popular size for
    hiking. The weight penalty over a comparable solo tent is often only a few
    hundred grams, and you can share the load with a hiking partner by splitting
    the poles and fly.
  </p>

  <h2>Lightweight hiking tents: how much should a 2 person tent weigh?</h2>
  <p>
    Weight is the trade-off you feel on every climb. Listings quote either
    <strong>minimum weight</strong> (tent, fly and poles only) or
    <strong>packed weight</strong> (including pegs, stuff sacks and guy lines). Compare
    like with like.
  </p>
  <figure class="guide-figure">
    <div class="guide-table-wrap">
      <table class="guide-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Class</th><th>2-person weight</th><th>Trade-offs</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Ultralight</td>
            <td>Under 1.5 kg</td>
            <td>Thinner fabrics, often trekking-pole pitched, higher price</td>
