    </div>
    <figcaption>Hiking tent weight classes for two-person tents.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    For a first tent, the lightweight band is usually the best value. Our <a
      href="/gear/bisinna-2-person-tent/">BISINNA 2-Person Tent review</a
    > looks at a budget freestanding option with a seller-claimed packed weight of
    about 2.16 kg.
  </p>

  <h2>Are ultralight tents worth it in Australia?</h2>
  <p>
    Ultralight tents save real weight, which matters on long multi-day walks.
    They usually get there with thin fabrics, fewer poles and designs that pitch
    with trekking poles. That makes them less forgiving: they need good peg
    placement, careful site choice and more practice. Consider one once you know
    you enjoy overnight hiking and have learned how to pitch in wind.
  </p>

  <h2>Freestanding vs trekking-pole tents: which should you choose?</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>freestanding</strong> tent holds its shape using its own poles, so you
    can pitch it on timber platforms, rock or hard ground where pegs struggle, and
    move it before staking it out. The <a href="/gear/naturehike-cloud-up-tent/"
      >Naturehike Cloud Up</a
    > is a lighter freestanding example. Many Australian campsites have hard ground
    or platforms, which makes freestanding tents very practical.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>trekking-pole</strong> or non-freestanding tent relies on pegs and tension
    to stand. It saves weight by using poles you already carry but takes more skill
    to pitch well and can be awkward on platforms.
  </p>

  <h2>Single wall vs double wall tents: which is better?</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>double-wall</strong> tent has a breathable inner tent and a separate
    waterproof fly. Condensation collects on the underside of the fly, away from the
    inner tent, and you can often pitch the inner alone on hot, dry nights for airflow
    and insect protection.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>single-wall</strong> tent is one waterproof layer. It is lighter and
    faster to pitch but more prone to condensation dripping inside, especially on
    still, humid nights. For a first tent, double-wall is the more forgiving choice.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    For a first tent, easy to pitch and dry inside beats a few hundred grams
    saved.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What floor space and layout does a backpacking tent need?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Floor length:</strong> check the floor suits your height, with room
      so your bag does not touch the walls.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Doors:</strong> two side doors let two people get in and out without
      climbing over each other.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Vestibules:</strong> covered space outside the inner for packs and boots.
      Two vestibules are a big comfort upgrade.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Peak height:</strong> matters if you want to sit up and change clothes
      inside.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Do you need a three-season or four-season tent?</h2>
  <p>
    A three-season tent is designed for spring, summer and autumn, handling
    rain, wind and occasional light snow. It covers the vast majority of
    Australian hiking. Four-season tents are built for heavy snow loads and
    strong alpine winds, and they are heavier, warmer and pricier. Unless you
    plan winter trips above the snowline, a well-pitched three-season tent is
    the right choice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whatever you choose, check the fly's waterproof rating, how low it sits to
    the ground in wind-driven rain, and whether the tent has enough guy-out
    points to stay taut in a blow.
  </p>

  <h2>How much should you spend on a first hiking tent?</h2>
  <p>
    If you will hike overnight regularly, spending more on a lighter, better
    made tent pays back quickly. If you are not sure yet, borrow a tent or
    choose a budget option and upgrade once you know what you want. Buying from
    brands with local distribution can also make warranty claims and spare parts
    easier.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/#kits">beginner hiking kits</a> include a tent chosen to balance
    price and weight, and <a href="/build-my-kit">Build My Kit</a> helps if you already
    own some gear. Pair the tent with a sleeping bag chosen using our <a
      href="/guides/sleeping-bag-temperature-ratings"
      >temperature ratings guide</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Choose a two-person, freestanding, double-wall, three-season tent in the
    1.5-2.5 kg range for your first tent. Check the floor size, doors and
    vestibules, compare weights on the same basis, and practise pitching it
    before you rely on it.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>Is a 2 person tent big enough for two hikers?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes, but snugly. Two people fit with packs stored in the vestibules. If
        you want more room or are tall, look at wider floors or a three-person
        tent.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What is a good weight for a 2 person hiking tent?</summary>
      <p>
        Around 1.5-2.5 kg (packed weight) is a good balance of weight,
        durability and price for most overnight hikers. Under 1.5 kg is
        ultralight territory.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Should a solo hiker buy a 1 or 2 person tent?</summary>
      <p>
        Many solo hikers prefer a two-person tent for the extra room to store a
        pack and wet gear inside. A one-person tent saves weight if you are
        happy with less space.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >What is the difference between a hiking tent and a camping tent?</summary
      >
      <p>
        Hiking or backpacking tents are designed to be carried, so they
        prioritise low weight and small packed size. Camping tents for car-based
        trips can be much heavier and roomier.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do I need a footprint for my hiking tent?</summary>
      <p>
        It is optional. A footprint protects the floor from abrasion and adds a
        little weight. A sheet of polycryo or Tyvek cut to size is a cheap
        alternative.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How do I stop condensation in my tent?</summary>
      <p>
        Pitch away from creeks and low hollows, open the vents, keep the fly
        taut and away from the inner, and store wet gear in the vestibule rather
        than inside.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
